    {%- when 'emerald' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--emerald">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>*<em>Are Emeralds naturally included? Yes. Natural Emeralds typically contain inclusions known as </em>jardin* — their internal “garden.” These are expected, and in a well-balanced stone, they add character rather than reduce beauty. A completely clean Emerald is unusual and should always be examined carefully.</li>
          <li>**Are Emeralds treated? Most natural Emeralds receive light to moderate colourless oiling, which is a traditional and widely accepted treatment. This should always be disclosed. We do not consider heavy resin, polymer, wax, acceptable for premium or astrological use.</li>
          <li>**Can Panna be worn daily? Yes — with mindful care and a secure setting. Emerald is suitable for regular wear, but it should be treated more gently than harder stones. Think of it as a gem with character, not one that responds well to force.</li>
          <li>**Which metal pairs best with Emerald? Gold is traditionally preferred for Emerald in Vedic astrology. Silver may be used only when specifically advised based on the horoscope.</li>
          <li>**Does Emerald change colour over time? No. Natural Emeralds do not “lose” their colour. If a stone appears to change, it is usually because stabilising oils have been affected by heat, chemicals, or harsh cleaning — not because the gem itself is unstable.</li>
          <li>**What is the ideal weight for astrological use? Typically 2–7 carats, depending on the chart and the strength of Mercury. As always, quality, colour presence, and suitability matter more than size alone.</li>
          <li>**Is Emerald suitable for astrological use? Yes — when it is appropriate for the wearer’s horoscope and worn specifically to support Mercury (Budh). Suitability should always be confirmed before wearing.</li>
          <li>**How do I verify a genuine Emerald? Ask for a third-party lab certificate that confirms: natural Emerald, treatment type disclosed, no resin or polymer filling, and online verification available. If a seller is vague about treatment or origin, that is a red flag.</li>
          <li>**Is a flawless Emerald always better? No. Emeralds are expected to have inclusions. A stone with lively colour, good structural balance, and honest natural character is often far more valuable than one that appears unnaturally perfect.</li>
          <li>**How is Emerald different from other Mercury stones? Emerald (Panna) is the primary gemstone of Mercury and the strongest Budh ratna. It works more directly and more distinctly than substitute stones, which tend to act more gently. This is why proper consultation before wearing is essential.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    {%- when 'blue-sapphire' -%}
