    {%- when 'red-coral' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--red_coral">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Is Red Coral a gemstone or an organic material?</strong> It is an organic gemstone, formed from marine life rather than mineral deposits — which is what gives it its distinct character, care requirements, and astrological significance.</p>
        <p><strong>Does Red Coral wear out with use?</strong> Mild surface wear is natural over time and is not a cause for concern. It does not reduce astrological effectiveness. Professional repolishing can restore surface finish when needed.</p>
        <p><strong>Is fake Coral common in the market?</strong> Yes — more so than almost any other gemstone. Dyed, plastic, ceramic, resin, and reconstructed coral are widely sold. Always insist on independent certification before purchasing.</p>
        <p><strong>Which Coral is best for astrology?</strong> Italian (Sardinian) Red Coral is the traditional benchmark. Japanese (Aka) Red Coral is the refined premium choice. Both are natural, untreated, and well-suited for Mangal remedies.</p>
        <p><strong>Are ceramic or plastic Corals suitable for astrology?</strong> No. They carry no astrological value, wear quickly, and are a straightforward misrepresentation of what natural coral is.</p>
        <p><strong>Can Red Coral be worn daily?</strong> Yes, with mindful care. Rotating wear helps preserve the surface polish, and removing it before physical activity extends its longevity significantly.</p>
        <p><strong>Which metal is recommended?</strong> Gold or Copper traditionally. Silver only when astrologically advised.</p>
        <p><strong>Does surface wear reduce astrological effect?</strong> No. Minor physical wear over time does not diminish the astrological role of natural coral — only the quality and authenticity of the stone itself determines that.</p>
        <p><strong>Why is some Red Coral very cheap?</strong> Almost always because it is imitation, dyed, plastic, resin-based, or reconstructed material being sold as natural coral. True Red Coral value lives in natural origin, density, and honest disclosure.</p>
        <h3>The PreciousCarats Promise</h3>
        <p>Every Red Coral we carry is natural, untreated, and ocean-origin — selected specifically for density, durability, and astrological suitability. Fully disclosed, independently certified, and chosen with the same standard we apply to every stone in our collection.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'pearl' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--pearl">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p><strong>Is Pearl a gemstone or an organic material?</strong> Pearl is an organic gemstone — formed naturally inside a mollusk over time. That origin is precisely what gives it its character, its care requirements, and its astrological significance.</p>
        <p><strong>Does Pearl wear out with use?</strong> A gradual, subtle loss of lustre is natural over years of wear — this is the nature of an organic gem and is entirely expected. It does not reduce astrological effectiveness.</p>
        <p><strong>Does surface wear reduce Pearl's astrological effect?</strong> No. Minor physical wear over time does not diminish the Moon's influence through the stone, provided the Pearl is natural and intact.</p>
        <p><strong>Which Pearl is best for astrology?</strong> Natural Basra Pearl is the most preferred. High-quality, undyed South Sea Pearl is widely accepted. Freshwater Pearls require careful selection. Dyed or imitation pearls carry no astrological value.</p>
        <p><strong>Are cultured pearls acceptable?</strong> Yes — when they are high-quality, thick-nacre, and undyed. Cultured pearls are widely accepted for astrological use when natural pearls are unavailable or outside budget.</p>
        <p><strong>Can Pearl be worn daily?</strong> Yes, with mindful care. Rotating wear and wiping after each use will preserve the lustre significantly over time.</p>
        <p><strong>Which metal should Pearl be set in?</strong> Silver traditionally. Gold when astrologically advised.</p>
        <p><strong>How can I tell if a Pearl is genuine?</strong> Insist on an independent third-party lab certificate confirming natural or cultured Pearl, with no dye or coating, verifiable online. A genuine pearl will also feel slightly gritty if rubbed gently against the teeth — a smooth, glassy feel suggests imitation.</p>
        <p><strong>Why are some Pearls very cheap?</strong> Almost always plastic, glass, or shell imitations, or thin-nacre cultured pearls that will not hold their lustre. The difference becomes visible within months of wear.</p>
        <h3>The PreciousCarats Promise</h3>
        <p>Every Pearl we carry is selected for lustre depth and nacre quality — astrologically suitable, naturally formed, and free from artificial shine or cosmetic enhancement. Fully disclosed, independently certified, and chosen with the same standard applied to every stone in our collection.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'hessonite' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--hessonite">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p><strong>What exactly is Hessonite?</strong> Hessonite is a variety of grossular garnet — a natural crystalline gemstone, not an organic material like Pearl or Coral — traditionally worn as a Vedic remedy for ☊ Rahu.</p>
        <p><strong>Is Hessonite always brown?</strong> No. Natural Hessonite ranges from honey and amber through cinnamon to cognac. Very dark or muddy stones are typically lower quality. The finest stones have a warm, earthy glow that feels alive rather than flat.</p>
        <p><strong>Is Hessonite usually treated?</strong> Most quality Hessonite is untreated. Light heat treatment may occur and must always be disclosed. We carry only stones with full treatment transparency.</p>
        <p><strong>Can it be worn daily?</strong> Yes. Hessonite is durable enough for regular daily wear when properly set and cared for.</p>
        <p><strong>Does it break easily?</strong> No — but like all gemstones, it can crack under strong impact or in a poorly made setting. A secure setting is important.</p>
        <p><strong>Which metal should it be set in?</strong> Silver is traditionally preferred. Gold may be used when astrologically advised.</p>
        <p><strong>Can Hessonite be worn with other gemstones?</strong> Yes — but combinations should always be guided by the individual chart. Do not combine gemstones without proper astrological assessment.</p>
        <p><strong>How soon do effects show?</strong> Typically within 30–60 days, depending on Rahu's placement and the specifics of the individual horoscope.</p>
        <p><strong>How do I verify a genuine Hessonite?</strong> Insist on an independent third-party lab certificate confirming natural Hessonite garnet, with treatment clearly disclosed and no glass filling, verifiable online.</p>
        <p><strong>Why is some Hessonite very cheap?</strong> Poor colour, dull appearance, synthetic or misrepresented material, or undisclosed treatments — almost always one or more of these. Quality Hessonite with genuine warmth and glow is not a commodity.</p>
        <h3>The PreciousCarats Promise</h3>
        <p>Every Hessonite we carry is selected for colour warmth and internal glow — chosen for astrological integrity, free from artificial brilliance or hidden enhancement, fully disclosed, and independently certified.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'cats-eye' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--cats_eye">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <p><strong>What exactly is Cat's Eye?</strong> A gemstone exhibiting chatoyancy — a moving band of reflected light caused by parallel needle-like inclusions within the stone. In Vedic astrology, Chrysoberyl Cat's Eye is the classical choice for ☋ Ketu.</p>
        <p><strong>Are all Cat's Eye stones astrologically effective?</strong> No. Only Chrysoberyl Cat's Eye is the true Lehsunia. Quartz Cat's Eye has limited, chart-dependent use. Fibre-optic, glass, and synthetic versions have no astrological value.</p>
        <p><strong>What colour should a good Cat's Eye be?</strong> Colour is secondary to eye quality. For Chrysoberyl, honey-green to yellowish tones are preferred. The eye's sharpness, continuity, and contrast matter far more than body colour alone.</p>
        <p><strong>Is Cat's Eye risky to wear?</strong> Yes — if worn without proper guidance. Ketu is a powerful and unpredictable influence. This stone should never be worn casually or on assumption. Always consult an experienced astrologer first.</p>
      </div>
    {%- when 'amethyst' -%}
      <div class="gem-tab gem-tab--amethyst">
        <p>Frequently Asked Questions</p>
        <p>Is Amethyst a natural gemstone? Yes. It is a natural variety of quartz, formed in the earth and not synthetic or composite.</p>
        <p>Does Amethyst fade over time? High-quality natural Amethyst is colour-stable under ordinary wear. Prolonged exposure to heat or strong direct sunlight may gradually lighten the colour — normal daily wear and storage does not pose this risk.</p>
        <p>Is Amethyst usually treated? Most Amethyst is natural and untreated. Confirm this through independent certification before purchasing.</p>
        <p>Is darker always better? No. The best stones show balanced, even colour with good clarity — rich without being so dark that the stone loses transparency and life.</p>
        <p>Can it be worn daily? Yes. At Mohs 7, Amethyst is suitable for daily wear when properly set. It is softer than the corundum and chrysoberyl stones in this collection, so a protective setting is advisable for rings worn during physical activity.</p>
        <p>Is Amethyst used in healing or spiritual practice? Yes — it is widely associated with calm, focus, emotional balance, and meditative clarity, and has been used in contemplative traditions across cultures for centuries.</p>
        <p>Does origin matter? Origin affects tone and character — African tends toward deeper purple, Brazilian toward softer violet — but colour, clarity, and cut quality matter more than the origin label alone.</p>
        <p>Which metal pairs best? Silver and white metals are commonly preferred and complement the stone's violet tones naturally. Gold may also be used when preferred or astrologically advised.</p>
        <p>Why is some Amethyst very cheap? Pale or low-grade colour, poor cut, or imitation and misrepresented material. Quality Amethyst with even saturation and good clarity is not a commodity — the difference is visible and meaningful.</p>
        <p>The PreciousCarats Promise</p>
        <p>Every Amethyst we carry is selected for natural colour depth and clarity — no artificial dyeing, no misrepresentation, fully disclosed and quality-checked before it reaches you.</p>
