        <p>Frequently Asked Questions</p>
        <p>Is Tourmaline a natural gemstone? Yes. Tourmaline is a naturally occurring gemstone family known for its extraordinary colour range and natural crystal forms — one of the most diverse in the mineral world.</p>
        <p>Is it usually treated? Most Tourmaline is untreated. Gentle heat treatment is used in some cases and is acceptable when fully disclosed. Always confirm before purchasing.</p>
        <p>Which colours are most valuable? Fine Indicolite (blue), vivid green, and quality Rubellite (red/pink) command the highest prices. Bi-colour and Watermelon Tourmalines are prized more for their uniqueness and rarity of form than for colour intensity alone.</p>
        <p>Can it be worn daily? Yes. At Mohs 7–7.5, Tourmaline is suitable for regular daily wear when properly set. A protective setting is advisable for rings worn during physical activity.</p>
        <p>Is Tourmaline used for astrology? Tourmaline is not a primary Navratna gemstone. It is worn for balance, grounding, and emotional ease, and sometimes chosen as a gentler colour-based alternative to stronger planetary stones when those have been advised against or feel too intense.</p>
        <p>Which metal pairs best? Silver and white metals suit the cooler tones — blue, green, bi-colour. Gold complements the warmer stones — pink, red, and golden varieties. Final choice may depend on the specific colour of the stone and personal or astrological preference.</p>
